This is a documentation excerpt, not a measurement -- no code was run, nothing
was compiled, nothing touched hardware. It is a verbatim read of a config file
already shipped inside C:\Program Files\RyzenAI\1.7.1, extracted from the
`waic-1.7.1-py3-none-any.whl` wheel (installed into the `ryzen-ai-1.7.1` conda
env as package `waic`), member path
`OGOAT/Collaterals/device.yaml`. Recorded here because it is the primary-source
answer to "does XDNA1 support int8/int16/bfloat16" -- a question this repo had
previously only answered at the toolchain level (Quark's config surface),
never at the silicon level. See RESEARCH.md's open questions, "Custom C++ XRT
/ hand-written AIE kernels" for the full context.

`device.yaml` is part of AMD's OGOAT graph-optimizer/cost-model library
(shared infrastructure for tiling and scheduling across AIE-based products,
not specific to the CNN-inference path this repo otherwise uses). It defines
one architecture block per AIE generation (AIE2, AIE2p, TestHW) and one
per-product device block that mixes in an architecture block by name. The
`phoenix:` block below is this project's own chip (Hawk Point is not named
separately -- see caveat at the bottom).

=== AIE2 architecture block (mixed into `phoenix:`) ===
  adds_per_cycle:
    bfloat16: 32
    int16: 32
    int8: 64
    int4: 64
  macs_per_cycle:
    bfloat16xbfloat16: 128
    int16xint8: 128
    int8xint8: 256
  core_data_memory: 64 (KB, per tile)
  core_num_banks: 4
  core_program_memory: 16 (KB)
  memtile_capacity: 512 (KB)
  memtile_num_banks: 8
  max_stream_bw: 4

=== phoenix: device block ===
  num_columns: 4
  num_rows: 4
  memtile_rows: 1
  dram_read_bandwidth: 16
  dram_write_bandwidth: 16
  <<: *AIE2

=== AIE2p architecture block (Strix -- mixed into `strix:`/`gpt2:`), for contrast ===
  adds_per_cycle:
    bfloat16: 32
    bfp16: 32
    int16: 32
    int8: 64
    int4: 64
  macs_per_cycle:
    bfloat16xbfloat16: 64
    bfp16xbfp16: 512
    int16xint16: 128
    int16xint8: 256
    int16xint4: 256
    int16xint2: 256
    int8xint16: 256
    int8xint8: 512
    int8xint4: 512

=== Reading this ===
- AIE2 (Phoenix/Hawk Point, this repo's hardware) natively supports bfloat16,
  int16, int8, and int4 arithmetic at the tile level, with real per-cycle MAC
  throughput numbers for bfloat16xbfloat16, int16xint8, and int8xint8. This is
  a citable, AMD-authored, in-SDK confirmation that the *silicon* is not the
  reason this repo's toolchain is INT8-only -- that limit (docs/DECISIONS.md
  locked #3, README.md's "X1 backend is XINT8 or nothing") is a Quark/VitisAI-EP
  config-surface restriction, not a hardware one.
- AIE2 is missing entries AIE2p has: no int16xint16, no int8xint4/int8xint16,
  no bfp16 (block floating point). This asymmetry is what makes the table
  credible as a real per-architecture spec rather than a copy-pasted default --
  Strix's AIE2p block is a strict superset in dtype combinations and (for most
  combinations) roughly double the throughput.
- int16xint8 at 128 macs/cycle is exactly the shape Quark's `A16W8` config asks
  for (INT16 activations, INT8 weights) -- see results/a16w8/. That measurement
  found 0/394 nodes reaching the NPU, traced to an opset-17-vs-21 ONNX Q/DQ
  domain issue in the VitisAI EP's graph matcher. This file confirms that
  finding was correctly scoped to the EP, not the hardware: the tile has a
  real int16xint8 MAC path, the EP just doesn't route to it via this config.

=== Caveat ===
This file is generic OGOAT infrastructure, not proof any particular kernel
compiles or runs for Phoenix. A separate, larger source tree bundled in the
same `waic` wheel (`aie4_models/`, a full internal AMD kernel-build repo with
per-dtype Conv/GEMM/MaxPool/GroupNorm/Softmax/etc. kernel source in int8x8,
int16x8, int16x16, and bf16 variants) was checked as a possible way to actually
build and run one of these kernels -- and is a dead end for this hardware:
`aie4_models/buildscripts/build_conv.py` hardcodes `set_dev_gen(DevGen.Aie4)`
at import time, `model_cfg.yaml`'s only documented `device:` choices are `mds`
(Medusa) and `swv` (SoundWave) -- both map to a `TestHW` architecture block in
device.yaml with no `macs_per_cycle` table at all, i.e. pre-silicon test
hardware, not a shipping AIE2/AIE2p product -- and `settings.sh` references
AMD-internal build infrastructure (`/proj/primebuilds/...`, `aiengine-eng`
license servers). This is an internal AMD engineering source tree for a
different, unreleased chip generation, incidentally bundled into a public
wheel; it is not a path to a custom kernel for this project's Phoenix/Hawk
Point hardware. Not attempted, for this reason -- confirmed from the source
before trying, not assumed.

Also correcting an overclaim from the previous commit (8cb20fc, not yet pushed):
that commit and RESEARCH.md said `vaie_overlay` ships `mesimulator`, `xchesscc`/
`xchessmk`, `xclbinutil`, `bootgen`, and `iss_dbg`. Re-checked the wheel's own
RECORD: it contains exactly two executables, `aiecompiler.exe` and
`graph_preprocessor.exe` (plus their .bat wrappers and a client DLL). Those
other names come from `vaie_overlay/cli.py`'s `__all__` list, which is a shared
entrypoint list used across AMD's Vitis Python packaging convention -- not a
manifest of what this specific wheel contains. Whether those tools exist
elsewhere in the 1.7.1 install is unconfirmed, not assumed present. README.md
and RESEARCH.md are corrected in the same commit as this file.
